<description>
Stamp the whole input pattern into output block slots marked by its unique most frequent color.

Input structure
- The input is a 3x3 grid of non-black colors.
- Exactly one color appears more often than every other color.

Rule
- Treat the input as both a source sprite and a 3x3 placement mask.
- Create a 9x9 black output divided into 3x3 block slots.
- For each input cell whose color is the unique most frequent color, copy
  the entire input grid into the corresponding output block slot.

Key insight
- The dominant color does not get expanded by itself; its positions select
  where full copies of the original 3x3 pattern are stamped.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "3x3 input pixel grid, with the unique most frequent color acting as a placement mask",
  "transform": "copy the whole input grid into each corresponding 3x3 output block whose source pixel has the mask color; leave all other blocks black",
  "grid_shape": "9x9 block grid"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 3x3 input, 9x9 output
  colors: all 9 cells non-black (colors 1-9 only)
  distinct_colors: 3-5
  dominant_color:
    count: 3-4 out of 9 cells, strictly more than any other single color
    uniqueness: exactly one color has the highest frequency (no ties)
  non_dominant_colors: each appears 1-2 times
  transform: |
    Create 9x9 black output divided into a 3x3 grid of 3x3 block slots.
    For each cell in the input whose color equals the dominant color,
    stamp the entire 3x3 input pattern into the corresponding block slot.
    All other block slots remain black.

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (input is small and fully random; constraints are easy to satisfy)
  steps:
    1. Pick K = randint(3, 5) distinct non-black colors (from 1-9).
    2. Pick one as the dominant color. Assign it D = 3 or 4 cells out of 9.
    3. Fill the remaining 9-D cells with the other K-1 colors, ensuring each
       non-dominant color appears at least once and no non-dominant color
       reaches D occurrences (to keep dominant strictly most frequent).
    4. Shuffle all 9 values into a 3x3 grid.
    5. Verify dominant color is strictly unique most-frequent. Retry if not.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

